#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119589 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119775 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "74764043-e1e7-56d6-8f53-9d287a30e538" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Determinant After Switching Two Rows"}] :question/id #uuid "596aa514-d598-5ffe-affe-f5580d130bd0" :question/math-academy-id "e-4422" :question/problem "Suppose $A$ is a $3 \\times 3$ matrix and $\\det (A) = -16.$ The matrix $B$ is obtained from $A$ by applying the elementary row operation $R_1 \\leftrightarrow R_2$ (switching the first and the second rows). What is the value of $\\det(B)?$" :question/worked-solution "Since swapping any two rows changes the sign of the determinant to the opposite sign, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(B) & =-det(A) \\\\  & =-(-16) \\\\  & =16.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119590 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119776 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2f11b223-edd2-59b8-b761-164d56e29746" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Determinant After Multiplying a Row by a Number"}] :question/id #uuid "89152146-8f04-5b4f-8436-15b82431c35d" :question/math-academy-id "e-4423" :question/problem "Suppose $A$ is a $6 \\times 6$ matrix and $\\det(A) = 32.$ The matrix $B$ is obtained from $A$ by applying the elementary row operation $R_3:= \\dfrac{1}{8} R_3$. What is the value of $\\det(B)?$" :question/worked-solution "The row operation applied to $A$ consists of multiplying a row by the number $\\dfrac{1}{8}.$\n\nWhen we do this, the determinant is multiplied by the same number. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(B) & =\\frac{1}{8}det(A) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}\\cdot 32 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119591 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119777 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "24e5e193-5e30-5480-9e5d-b7b8f1d5de87" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Determinant After Applying Several Elementary Operations"}] :question/id #uuid "6d46c442-4675-5e0e-a834-a8b10ad2b86a" :question/math-academy-id "e-4424" :question/problem "Suppose $A$ is an $8 \\times 8$ matrix and $\\det(A) = 5.$ The matrix $B$ is obtained from $A$ by applying the elementary row operations $R_4 \\leftrightarrow R_5,$ $R_6:= 4 R_6,$ and $R_8: = R_8 - \\dfrac{1}{2} R_7.$ What is the value of $\\det(B)?$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's figure out what each operation does to the determinant.\n\n- The operation $R_4 \\leftrightarrow R_5$ involves swapping two rows, so it changes the sign of the determinant to the opposite one.\n\n- The operation $R_6:= 4 R_6$ involves multiplying a row by $4,$ so the determinant is multiplied by $4$ as well.\n\n- The operation $R_8:= R_8 - \\dfrac{1}{2} R_7$ involves adding a multiple of one row to another row,  so it does not change the determinant.\n\nTherefore, we have to change the sign of the determinant to the opposite one and multiply by $4$ as well. Consequently, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(B) & =4\\cdot (-det(A)) \\\\  & =-4det(A) \\\\  & =-4\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =-20.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119665 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119850 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9a0ab53e-79ff-5520-bb50-069fd381d29d" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Row Operation Given the Connection Between Two Determinants"}] :question/id #uuid "cbd9b4bc-146a-57f7-a03f-f045f86f3c21" :question/math-academy-id "e-6143" :question/problem "Given that $\\det(X)=\\det(Y),$ which elementary row operations transforms matrix $X$ to matrix $Y,$ where\n\n![](images/38/3813c34b623ef9ad6f7ca534fb3a6e373d4ce1961dab49dfab9e414fea84daae.svg)" :question/worked-solution "Since $\\det(X)=\\det(Y)$, the row operation applied to $X$ must be to add or subtract a multiple of one row to another row. In other words, it is of the form \n\n$$\nR_j := R_j + (k)R_i, \\quad i \\neq j.\n$$\n\nBy considering the given row operations that are of the above form, we notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(X) & =|\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 0 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}| & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+3{R}_{3} \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =det(Y).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the required row operation is\n\n$$\nR_2:= R_2 + 3R_3.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119666 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119851 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "272d9a54-ba97-5175-86df-0a78337da7b9" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Determinant After Multiplying a Matrix by a Scalar"}] :question/id #uuid "ce910ef2-c3af-5173-84d4-ee2a4c957954" :question/math-academy-id "e-6148" :question/problem "Suppose $A$ is a $4\\times4$ matrix and $\\det(A)=5.$ What is the value of $\\det(2A)?$" :question/worked-solution "Multiplying a $n\\times n$ matrix by a scalar $k$ is equivalent to multiplying each row by $k,$ and this occurs $n$ times. This leads to the result\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(k\\cdot A)={k}^{n}\\cdot det(A).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $k=2$ and $n=4.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(2A) & ={2}^{4}\\cdot det(A) \\\\  & =16\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =80.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265957 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119775 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "74764043-e1e7-56d6-8f53-9d287a30e538" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Determinant After Switching Two Rows"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2528ad02-5f9f-56ea-8410-d39c904636dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263319} {:answer/id #uuid "362ac5c7-47e1-5e9d-8da7-99af33e68594" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "17" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263480} {:answer/id #uuid "4fa78b20-3f5d-55c8-86ed-4c78587bb1c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 175